# Establish the playable Unreal runtime foundation before world content

Implement the target-side runtime contracts before finalizing content assets.
Use the preserved behavior reference and source evidence to define lifecycle,
state, timing, input, physics, camera, event, and ownership semantics in Unreal.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Establish the gameplay framework, game flow, event boundaries, ownership,
  timing, and reset/lifecycle rules required by later systems.
- [ ] Record behavioral and mathematical contracts before implementing each
  source-derived runtime system.
- [ ] Keep runtime code independent from final character, vehicle, material, and
  animation asset quality wherever stable typed interfaces are sufficient.
- [ ] Provide synthetic or representative fixtures for runtime tests so missing
  final art cannot block code-level behavioral validation.
- [ ] Use source/reference evidence rather than asset appearance to choose
  gameplay behavior, units, thresholds, or state transitions.

## Runtime inventory checkpoint — 2026-09-13

The Unreal project already has substantial code-first infrastructure.
`SharApplication`, `SharWorld`, `SharLoading`, `SharMessaging`, `SharCamera`,
`SharInteraction`, `SharMissions`, `SharProgression`, `SharSave`, `SharUI`, and
`SharVehicles` provide typed state and subsystem boundaries that should be
completed rather than replaced.

The executable bootstrap now selects the composition-root `SharGameInstance`
and the project-owned Game Mode, Game State, Player Controller, and neutral
player Pawn. Runtime composition now binds world readiness, per-local-player
input leases, and ordered source-exit/target-entry lifecycle evidence to
application-mode
commit. Production content definitions and the services that execute each
lifecycle plan remain separate authorities.

The former duplicate startup state authority has been removed.
`SharGameInstance` is now a state-free composition shell, while
`SharApplicationModeCoordinator` is the sole application-mode authority for
queued transition, readiness, commit, recovery, and revision state.

Reference behavior establishes the first lifecycle invariant: context changes
are requested before they are executed, and the transition boundary stops the
old context before starting the new one. Normal gameplay follows a loading
context, and active context controls input availability.

## First implementation slice

- [x] Select one application-mode authority and remove the independent
  GameInstance startup state machine.
- [x] Preserve requested-versus-committed transition semantics and ordered
  source exit before target entry.
- [x] Bind loading readiness, world readiness, and input policy to the gameplay
  transition without making asset availability the state-machine authority.
- [x] Add the minimal project-owned gameplay bootstrap classes required by
  Unreal, including the controller and representative player pawn boundary.
- [x] Prove `LoadingGameplay -> Gameplay -> Pause/Resume` lifecycle behavior in
  automation before building final player or vehicle presentation.

## Native gameplay framework bootstrap — 2026-09-13

The root `shar` module now owns `ASharGameMode`, `ASharGameState`,
`ASharPlayerController`, and a neutral `ASharPlayerPawn`. Project settings
select
the SHAR Game Mode globally, and its class defaults select the other
project-owned framework classes. The representative Pawn owns only a scene root,
does not tick, and cannot auto-possess a player or AI; movement, collision,
camera, mesh, animation, and input mapping remain deferred to validated avatar
and character policy.

`SHAR.Runtime.GameplayFramework.Bootstrap` verifies the configured global Game
Mode and all framework class defaults without depending on final assets.
`SHAR.Application.Transition.GameplayPauseResumeLifecycle` drives the real
application coordinator through `front_end -> loading_gameplay -> gameplay ->
pause -> gameplay`. Every step proves the source observation remains active
through preparation and readiness and changes only at commit, then completes and
releases its transaction.

World-readiness barriers and staged input leases are now correlated at the
root gameplay composition boundary. Pawn existence or possession still cannot
stand in for readiness, lifecycle-plan completion, or committed input authority.

## Per-local-player input leases — 2026-09-14

`SharInput` now provides `USharLocalPlayerInputSubsystem` as the first native
input authority scoped to one `ULocalPlayer`. A mapping-context lease stages
without changing native input, carries independent owner-mode and lease
revisions, rejects duplicate ownership, and activates only when commit can reach
a live `UEnhancedPlayerInput`. Release removes the mapping, and local-player
teardown releases any still-active contexts before subsystem destruction.

`SHAR.Input.LocalPlayer.LeaseLifecycle` uses synthetic mapping contexts with a
transient native local player and Player Controller. It proves staging has no
side effect, commit fails closed before Enhanced Player Input exists, retry can
activate the mapping at the declared priority, stale revisions cannot commit or
release, duplicate SHAR ownership is rejected, explicit release removes the
mapping, and local-player teardown removes an active mapping automatically.

World readiness and per-player input leases now carry the same application
transition revision. `loading_gameplay` can stage input without activation, and
the gameplay composition adapter activates it only after ordered lifecycle
handoff and application commit. Asset availability remains evidence, not
state-machine authority.

Before production startup can invoke the runtime bootstrap, the initial `entry`
observation must not invent world, profile, or session revisions. The entry
contract therefore uses absent authority (`NAME_None` plus empty revision
strings) until boot establishes real revisioned state. Synthetic `*_none*`
revisions are test fixtures, not production identities. `USharRuntimeBootstrap`
now derives the initial mode identity and revision from the loaded `Entry`
definition rather than accepting an externally constructed observation.

## Correlated gameplay readiness — 2026-09-14

The root `USharGameplayTransitionComposer` now correlates Application,
Loading,
World Readiness, and per-local-player Input without owning parallel mode state.
Application requests publish `WorldId`; world barriers and input leases carry
the same transition revision used by the load request. Read-only snapshots let
the composition root verify each authority rather than trust duplicate claims.

Input now exposes a side-effect-free commit-readiness preflight. Loading can
stage a gameplay mapping, but the mapping remains absent from Enhanced Input
until the application coordinator commits gameplay. The composer then activates
input, publishes loading success, and completes the application transition.
Partial post-commit input failure releases active mappings and follows the
target
mode's declared recovery path.

`SHAR.Runtime.GameplayTransition.ReadinessAndCommit` uses transient native
runtime objects and synthetic definitions only. It rejects a fully ready world
barrier from a stale transition without activating input, then proves the
correlated path keeps `loading_gameplay` authoritative through readiness and
activates gameplay input only after gameplay commit. World readiness separately
rejects checkpoint callbacks carrying a stale transition revision.

## Ordered application lifecycle handoff — 2026-09-14

`USharApplicationModeCoordinator` now consumes typed lifecycle completion
evidence for each transition. `SourceExit` must match the active source mode's
revision and `ExitPlanId`; `TargetEntry` must match the target mode's revision
and `EntryPlanId`. Target entry is dependency-blocked until source exit has been
accepted, and `Commit` rejects attempts to bypass either completion.

The active observation remains on the source through both handoff records and
changes only when commit succeeds. Once source-exit completion is accepted,
ordinary pre-commit cancellation is no longer safe: a subsequent failure follows
the target's declared recovery mode when one exists because source effects may
already have been released. Terminal shutdown targets retain their explicit
fatal result instead of inventing recovery.

`SHAR.Application.Transition.LifecycleOrdering` proves ordering, stale-evidence
fencing, duplicate rejection, source authority before commit, and target
publication only after both lifecycle records. A separate handoff recovery
regression proves failure after source exit enters recovery rather than
pretending the exited source is intact.

The root gameplay composer accepts those lifecycle records from its caller and
submits them in source-exit then target-entry order. It does not infer
completion
from plan identity or Asset Manager state and does not execute subsystem-owned
plan effects itself.

## World authority absence policy — 2026-09-15

Application world observations now follow the target mode's declared
`WorldPolicy` instead of requiring a fabricated world token in every non-entry
mode. `Prepare`, `Retain`, and `Own` require a canonical world identity and real
revision. `None` and `TearDown` require world identity and revision to be
absent.

The front-end test fixture no longer uses `no_gameplay_world` or
`sha256:world_none_v1`. Configuration accepts an absent front-end world while
rejecting those sentinel values. Transition submission also validates the target
mode policy, so a teardown target such as `exit` accepts absence and rejects a
fabricated replacement identity.

Recovery now reconciles the observation with the recovery mode's world policy.
Returning to `front_end` or `exit` clears the stale gameplay world identity and
revision rather than carrying destroyed world authority across the lifecycle
boundary. Profile absence remains separate policy work.

## Gameplay-session authority policy — 2026-09-15

Application session observations now follow a separate `SessionPolicy` instead
of forcing every non-entry mode to carry a fabricated gameplay-session revision.
`Prepare`, `Retain`, and `Own` require a real revision. `None` and `TearDown`
require absence, and sentinel revisions such as `sha256:session_none_v1` are
rejected.

The canonical runtime fixtures declare no gameplay session for `entry` or
`front_end`, preparation for `loading_gameplay`, ownership for `gameplay`,
retention for `pause`, and teardown for `exit`. Transition submission validates
the target session policy independently from world policy. Retained world and
session authority must exactly match the source observation rather than merely
carrying another syntactically valid revision.

Recovery to `front_end` or `exit` now clears stale gameplay-session authority in
addition to stale world authority.
`SHAR.Application.Configuration.SessionAuthorityPolicy` proves front-end and
teardown absence, loading and active session requirements, sentinel rejection,
and recovery cleanup. Profile
selection remains intentionally unchanged until its own authority policy is
modeled.

## Retained-authority graph fencing — 2026-09-15

Pause/resume authority is now fenced in both the active request path and the
catalog graph. A source with `Retain` world or gameplay-session policy cannot
transition directly to a `Retain` or `Own` target while substituting a different
identity or revision. Introducing a new authority requires a preparation or
teardown boundary.

Catalog activation also rejects any `Retain` target whose declared predecessor
does not publish the authority that target promises to retain. This prevents a
syntactically reachable but semantically impossible mode edge from becoming
active. `SHAR.Application.Catalog.GraphValidation` covers world and session
predecessor mismatches, while the pause/resume lifecycle regression rejects
replacement world and session revisions before accepting the real resume.

The preserved behavior evidence is that ordinary pause keeps the active level,
and gameplay startup performs fresh level-owned initialization only when entered
from loading rather than when returning from pause. The modern contract
therefore models resume as authority continuity, not a new world/session
acquisition.

The same submission boundary now binds both source and target mode revisions to
the loaded catalog definitions. Previously a caller could provide an arbitrary
well-formed target revision and then submit matching readiness/lifecycle
evidence; commit would publish that caller-owned revision. Submission now
returns `StaleRevision` before creating a transition snapshot whenever either
mode revision differs from the catalog-owned definition. The stale-evidence
Automation test covers rejection and preservation of the source observation.

Recovery references now fail closed during catalog activation as well. A
non-loading mode can no longer declare a canonical-looking but absent
`RecoveryModeId`; activation returns `RecoveryTargetMissing`. Modes that retain
world or gameplay-session authority also require an explicit recovery target at
definition validation, so retained authority never has an undeclared failure
route.

## Prepared-authority ownership handoff — 2026-09-15

The `loading_gameplay -> gameplay` boundary now preserves the exact world and
session authority prepared by loading. A target `Own` policy can promote
prepared, retained, or already-owned authority, but cannot substitute another
identity or revision during application commit. A new authority must enter
through an explicit target `Prepare` boundary.

Catalog validation applies the same rule structurally: every predecessor of a
`Retain` or `Own` world/session target must publish that authority. Synthetic
graph regressions cover invalid ownership edges, and the full pause/resume
lifecycle rejects replacement world and session revisions both when gameplay
claims prepared loading authority and when pause returns retained authority.

Preserved behavior evidence remains the loading/gameplay boundary itself: the
legacy loading context constructs the level-facing runtime while gameplay
performs its fresh level initialization only when entered from loading. The
modern ownership handoff therefore promotes the prepared world rather than
minting a different one at mode commit.

## Selected-profile authority policy — 2026-09-15

Application mode definitions now declare `ProfilePolicy` independently from
world and gameplay-session policy. The main lifecycle fixture follows the
contracted startup route `entry -> boot -> front_end`: entry has no selected
profile, boot prepares one concrete revision, and front end accepts that exact
revision as active profile authority. Gameplay loading, gameplay, and pause
retain it; exit tears it down.

Coordinator configuration and submission now validate profile presence from the
target mode policy rather than from a special-case mode-kind rule. A retaining
or owning target rejects a replacement profile revision, catalog activation
rejects profile ownership/retention edges whose predecessor has no profile
authority, and post-handoff recovery clears the profile only when the recovery
mode does not require it.

`SHAR.Application.Configuration.ProfileAuthorityPolicy` exercises startup
acquisition and the boot-to-front-end handoff, including replacement rejection.
Definition and graph Automation cover missing recovery for profile retention and
impossible profile predecessor edges. The root gameplay-transition fixture keeps
profile absent because that integration test owns only world, loading, and input
composition.

The preserved behavior basis is lifecycle continuity rather than a direct
legacy class translation: startup initializes durable game-data/save services,
and ordinary front-end, gameplay, and pause context changes do not reinitialize
that authority. Modern profile selection remains owned by profile/save services;
Application stores only the correlated revision needed for mode fencing.

## Recovery authority compatibility — 2026-09-15

Recovery after source-exit or target commit now fails closed on authority
requirements. Because the coordinator publishes recovery directly rather than
starting a second preparation transaction, a recovery mode cannot use `Prepare`
for world, gameplay-session, or selected-profile authority. Any authority it
requires must already exist in both possible observations: the failed target
and every permitted predecessor.

Catalog activation returns a typed recovery-authority mismatch for impossible
recovery graphs. The coordinator independently builds and validates a candidate
recovery observation before publication, so even corrupted post-activation
policy state cannot publish a mode that claims missing authority.

`SHAR.Application.Catalog.GraphValidation` covers recovery to an authority that
is absent before target commit and recovery that attempts new preparation.
`SHAR.Application.Transition.InvalidRecoveryAuthority` mutates transient test
policy after activation and proves runtime recovery still fails closed without
corrupting the prior observation.

Preserved behavior evidence remains lifecycle ordering: the legacy game-flow
boundary stops one context before starting another, while loading owns temporary
world-preparation state and suppresses gameplay input. Modern recovery therefore
cannot pretend that a new world/session/profile preparation happened merely by
changing the active mode identity.

## Root gameplay catalog foundation — 2026-09-13

The first code-first runtime slice now establishes the missing root catalog
boundary required by the application and loading coordinators. `SharContent`
provides `USharGameplayCatalog`, grouped Primary Asset identities, deterministic
validation, and `USharGameplayCatalogSubsystem` as the single accepted catalog
revision authority.

The Asset Manager now registers `SharCatalog` under
`/Game/SHAR/Data/Catalog`. Activation fails closed for invalid definitions,
rejects in-place replacement of an accepted revision, and exposes read-only
identity lookup without directory scanning or final presentation assets.

Validation evidence:

- `sharEditor Linux Development` built `SharContent` and the root runtime module
  with Unreal Header Tool and Clang successfully and no compiler warnings
  or errors;
- `SHAR.Content.GameplayCatalog.Activation` reached
  `Result={Success}` in headless NullRHI automation; and
- `SHAR.Content.GameplayCatalog.Validation` ran independently in headless
  NullRHI automation and reached `Result={Success}` with process exit code zero.

This slice intentionally does not implement alias-table resolution, bounded
asynchronous Asset Manager loading, bundle acquisition, or dependency-closure
loading yet. Those remain composition work before the application coordinator
can consume production mode definitions.

## Application authority consolidation — 2026-09-13

The second code-first slice removes the parallel startup state machine from
`SharUI`. `USharGameInstance` now lives in the root `shar` composition module,
contains no application-mode state, and owns only the root loading and bootstrap
adapters. `SharUI` returns to its frontend-only boundary.

`USharApplicationModeCatalogSubsystem` no longer accepts a free-form catalog
revision. It must bind to an active `USharGameplayCatalogSubsystem` and
inherit the accepted root revision.
It rejects any Application Mode whose Primary Asset ID
is not declared by the root `SharCatalog` application-mode family.

`USharRuntimeBootstrap` composes the root catalog, complete declared
Application Mode set, mode catalog, and coordinator exactly once. It rejects an
incomplete mode family before coordinator configuration, while the coordinator
remains the sole owner of active mode and transition state.

Validation is no longer compile-only.
`SHAR.Runtime.Bootstrap.ApplicationComposition`
executes in headless NullRHI Automation and proves a complete synthetic mode
family configures the catalog and coordinator exactly once with the loaded
`Entry` revision and absent world, profile, and session authorities.

Production Primary Asset loading now begins from the project game instance only
after Asset Manager completes its initial scan. `USharRuntimeAssetLoader`
requires exactly one `SharCatalog`, loads and activates it asynchronously, then
loads exactly the complete `SharApplicationMode` family declared by that root
catalog. Missing or ambiguous assets fail with typed startup results instead of
falling back to directory scans or synthetic production definitions.

The repository still contains no authored files beneath `/Game/SHAR/Data`, so
production startup cannot yet reach a loaded runtime catalog. Deterministic
generation of the root catalog and Application Mode Data Assets remains a
content pipeline prerequisite. Runtime composition of successfully loaded
definitions is no longer pending.

`USharRuntimeAssetLoader` now publishes one terminal result. `USharGameInstance`
subscribes before loading begins and composes only `Ready` into the existing
game-instance Application Mode catalog and coordinator subsystems. Loader
failure leaves those authorities not configured.

`SHAR.Runtime.AssetLoading.ApplicationComposition` uses a transient synthetic
Asset Manager inventory without creating packages. It proves exact catalog and
mode-family requests, deferred mode completion, stale catalog-callback
rejection, single terminal publication, and successful bootstrap into the
loaded `Entry`. `SHAR.Runtime.AssetLoading.Preflight` continues to prove missing
dependencies and zero root catalogs fail closed.

No successful production asset load is claimed before the generated Data Assets
exist. The next lifecycle-composition work remains correlating loading
readiness, world readiness, and per-local-player input leases with one
application transition revision.

Recovery identity hardening now keeps committed-failure observations coherent:
if a target recovers to a third mode, the coordinator publishes the recovery
definition's revision token instead of reusing the failed target revision.
Synthetic application fixtures now give every mode its own deterministic
revision, and a dedicated third-mode recovery regression covers the case.

